our shout out tonight for an unusual  collaboration between an artist in  Brooklyn working to honor a brave  journalist James Foley was 40 years old  when his career as a war zone photo  journalist was tragically and brutally  cut short too awful tragic news from  overseas late today it's about an  American journalist named James Foley  beheaded by members of the violent  Islamic group Isis he reported from  dangerous places bearing witness to the  lives of people a world away now almost  five years later an artist in Brooklyn  Bradley McCollum has made it his mission  to let the world finally see the work  that James Foley risk his life to create  when you searched his name all you would  find were the evidences of his execution  and it did seem to sort of brant  emboldened the executioner but did  little to tell his story  so with the permission of James Foley's  mother Diane  but Colin began to go through the video  and still photos that James had left  behind in his backpack that had never  been seen publicly and inside the  backpack for these five hard drives and  his old computer and at that point this  project became very personal using an  industrial-sized printer McCollum first  creates a silk overlay of the images he  has selected and then places the printed  fabric over a canvas painting he is made  of one of the scenes Foley captured with  his camera so for me this becomes this  moment of  almost like Christmas well the painting  behind me is one of my favorite  paintings in this series and it really  combines two images it's capturing what  I really think of is the kind of this  moment of beauty within a place of  absolute destruction and kind of ruins  and overlaying this oil painting of the  landscape is a detailed scan and  reprinting of a letter that Jim wrote  another one taken in Libya during the  overthrow of the Libyan leader Kadafi  what you see in the painting is a whole  series of rebel soldiers with their cell  phone cameras taking an image of this  fallen soldier we know now that is  Qaddafi's son dozens of images photos of  war turned into art for me it's not  important that you understand the sub  narratives I think as a painting it  begins to speak universally to to war  and conflict and accountability before  being taken hostage by Isis Foley had  earlier been held hostage by a group in  Libya which ultimately let him go but  not before putting him through a true  test of his courage I jumped up tried to  say we were journalists then I was hit  several times with lot of ak-47 punched  and dragged into a vehicle with my hands  tied behind my back  for Bradley McCollum there is a heavy  responsibility  to get it right and it is a  responsibility it's a responsibility to  to create work that both honors and and  takes the next step beyond simply being  a memorial but one that transforms and  makes us think in a much broader sense  so it is a tribute to his journalism oh  absolutely  yeah absolutely none of these paintings  would exist if Jim wasn't as committed  to telling the stories he told I'm not  using anyone elses images I'm looking at  and thinking about and making selections  based on the footage he took in a way it  feels often like I'm collaborating with  Jim and so our shout out tonight -  Bradley McCallum whose works of art  bring new life to the heroic journalism  of James Foley that's our program for  tonight thanks for joining us we'll see  you back here next week
 

